"""Original fictional/supervised introductory implementation practice."""
def build(c,m):
 return c(65,'Community Programme Implementation Practicum','Community Programme\nImplementation\nPracticum','Prepare, rehearse and review a fictional implementation plan with feasible responsibilities, safe participation, honest status records and a supervised learning boundary.',[
 m('From plan to feasible delivery','Translate a fictional programme purpose into bounded tasks and responsibilities.',[
'Implementation turns an approved plan into coordinated activity, but a classroom exercise is not approval to deliver. Begin with the intended purpose, the people the fictional programme is meant to serve and the assumptions linking activity to benefit. Separate tasks, outputs and outcomes: preparing a session is a task, holding it is an output and a meaningful change for people is an outcome requiring suitable evidence. The retained programme-development course provides the foundation for this distinction. IACD standards add the importance of collective planning and learning; neither a checklist nor attendance proves the intended benefit. [S31]',
'A feasible task needs an owner, a time, necessary resources and dependencies. Dependencies may include approved materials, a suitable venue, communication support, qualified supervision or an external decision. Show what cannot begin until the relevant condition is met. Do not mark a task complete because an email requesting approval was sent. OECD participation guidance connects resources, communication and access to a process\'s design. In the invented plan, identify which information is assumed for the exercise and which would need actual verification through authorised arrangements before real delivery. [S32]',
'Roles should match competence and authority. A learner can prepare an invented schedule and explain a concern; they do not gain authority to investigate complaints, recruit children, make legal determinations or independently deliver specialist services. The term practicum here means introductory fictional or supervised learning, not a completed field placement. Supervised skills observation would require a separate approved arrangement, suitable tasks and appropriate feedback. State these limits in the plan so the task cannot be mistaken for evidence of independent professional competence or an academy promise that a real placement has been completed. [S31]',
'Resources include paid and unpaid time, travel, materials, access arrangements and support for those coordinating the work. The Newham evaluation makes connecting roles and context visible but does not supply a universal staffing or cost-effectiveness formula. Do not assume that volunteers will absorb every shortfall, or treat their availability as confirmed without agreement. A smaller feasible activity may be preferable to an ambitious schedule built on unapproved contributions. Use invented roles and aggregate resource assumptions only; no real learner, volunteer, complainant, child or survivor records belong in the exercise. [R41, S31]'],
'An invented programme schedules two sessions before the venue, interpreter and supervisor are confirmed. The learner is listed as an independent safeguarding lead.',
'Create a task sequence with owners, dependencies, resource status and a corrected learner role boundary.',
'Hold dependent tasks as pending, distinguish requests from approvals and keep safeguarding with qualified arrangements. The plan is a simulation, not a placement record.', ['S31','S32','R41'],[
('Tasks outputs and outcomes are distinct.','A fictional team holds two sessions. What does that count show?','A delivery output, not proven development benefit.',['A causal improvement for all residents.','A completed professional placement.','A verified school certificate.'],'Activity completion does not establish outcomes.'),
('Dependencies should govern task status.','A venue request is sent but not accepted. What is the task status?','Awaiting confirmation before dependent delivery.',['Venue secured automatically.','Session completed.','All access arrangements verified.'],'A request is not an approval.'),
('Roles must match authority and competence.','An introductory learner is assigned independent safeguarding decisions. What correction is needed?','Use qualified arrangements and keep the learner within an approved bounded role.',['Treat the quiz score as authority.','Ask the class to investigate real cases.','Remove supervision to encourage independence.'],'Learning participation does not confer specialist authority.'),
('Practicum here is introductory simulated or supervised learning.','A fictional implementation worksheet is completed. What must not be claimed?','A completed real field placement or independent competence.',['A draft learning output.','A reasoned task sequence.','An identified supervision dependency.'],'The scope excludes unobserved field competence.'),
('Unpaid resources require feasible voluntary agreement.','A plan assigns unlimited volunteer hours. What should replace that assumption?','Bounded agreed contributions and a realistic contingency.',['A guarantee that goodwill covers all costs.','A compulsory contribution for unrelated benefits.','An invented receipt for donated work.'],'Resource planning needs honest limits.')]),
 m('Rehearsal exceptions and safe changes','Rehearse fictional delivery and assign qualified responses to exceptions.',[
'A rehearsal explores whether a planned sequence can work under stated conditions. Use an invented notice, schedule and set of roles, and clearly label all materials as simulated. A rehearsal can reveal missing information or a confusing handover; it cannot demonstrate that a real community received a service. OECD participation guidance supports clear communication and appropriate resources, while IACD standards make learning and reflection relevant. The exercise should have a defined purpose and a fictional alternative for a learner who prefers not to enact a role. No personal experience is needed to make the practice useful. [S32, S31]',
'Exceptions test a plan\'s assumptions. A venue may become unavailable, an access arrangement may fail or a volunteer may withdraw. Specify who can pause, revise or authorise a change, and how affected participants would receive appropriate information through approved arrangements. Do not improvise a larger activity to preserve a success claim when resources are missing. The Tanzanian qualitative study raises questions about contribution burdens and uncertain support, but does not prove that any particular contingency will restore cooperation. Use its limitations to resist an easy universal solution and keep the proposed response conditional. [R43]',
'A routine complaint about timing and a serious safeguarding concern require different routes. The World Bank project guidance provides a benchmark for accessible, impartial handling, not investigation competence. In the rehearsal, name the designated qualified dependency and stop at the learner\'s boundary; do not simulate a real distressing disclosure with an unprepared person. Avoid public mediation of allegations or promises of absolute confidentiality. A fictional pause can be the correct response when authority, competence or suitable arrangements are unavailable. Safety is not established merely because a practice session was completed without a recorded incident. [S33, S31]',
'Keep status changes traceable. Record the original assumption, the invented exception, the decision owner, the revised action and the reason. A proposed replacement venue is still proposed until the necessary access and approval conditions are met. Separate internal learning notes from any future authorised operational record, and never insert fabricated live receipts or signatures. This task rehearses transparent decisions under uncertainty rather than creating a record of actual service delivery. Supervised observation and local review remain pending, and no course score can replace the approval needed for a real programme change. [S32, S31]'],
'During an invented rehearsal, the accessible venue becomes unavailable. An organiser suggests an inaccessible replacement and asks the learner to sign a completed-delivery record.',
'Write an exception note with the failed assumption, pause decision, qualified owner, conditional alternative and honest simulation status.',
'Do not sign a false delivery record. Pause dependent activity, retain access requirements and record the alternative as conditional on appropriate verification and authority.', ['S32','S31','R43','S33'],[
('A rehearsal is not actual service delivery.','A team practises an invented schedule. How should its record be labelled?','Simulated rehearsal, not completed community delivery.',['Verified live staging.','Completed field placement.','Independent service accreditation.'],'The record must match the event that occurred.'),
('Exceptions need authorised change decisions.','An accessible venue becomes unavailable. What should happen before choosing a replacement?','Review access and authority, with a pause if conditions are unmet.',['Ignore the original access need.','Mark delivery successful anyway.','Assume every alternative is approved.'],'Contingencies must preserve relevant requirements.'),
('A fictional alternative supports safe learning.','A learner prefers not to act a role. What is suitable?','An equivalent invented written exercise without personal disclosure.',['A demand to describe personal trauma.','A requirement to recruit a real participant.','Loss of unrelated support.'],'Learning can assess reasoning without coercive participation.'),
('Safeguarding exceptions need qualified routes.','A rehearsal introduces a serious concern. What is the learner\'s appropriate boundary?','Recognise the designated qualified response dependency without investigation.',['Decide guilt in public.','Promise absolute secrecy.','Collect a real child\'s account as practice.'],'The exercise does not authorise specialist case handling.'),
('Status records should retain changes and reasons.','A replacement option is not approved. What belongs in the exception log?','Its conditional status, responsible owner and verification gap.',['A fabricated approval signature.','A claim the session already happened.','Only the final success label.'],'Traceable status prevents false completion claims.')]),
 m('Handover review and evidence limits','Prepare a responsible simulated handover and distinguish learning from certification.',[
'A handover identifies what is complete, what remains unresolved and who accepts responsibility for the next step. An invented implementation note can show tasks completed within the simulation, pending dependencies and proposed follow-up dates. Do not assume that sending a document transfers responsibility; acceptance and suitability need separate confirmation through authorised arrangements in real work. IACD standards connect organisation and learning, while OECD participation guidance highlights feedback on how input is used. The handover should help another role understand the actual status rather than present an unqualified success narrative. [S31, S32]',
'Review the plan against its purpose, access arrangements, resources and decision record. Distinguish the count of activities from who could use them and from the change the programme intended. If a fictional session had twelve planned places and nine simulated attendances, that is not evidence that all community needs were met. Missing data and absent perspectives remain relevant. Contextual surveys of registered youth-organisation officials cannot establish statewide effects or validate this plan; reporting inconsistencies and possible overlap between related papers further limit numerical teaching claims. Use evidence to sharpen questions rather than manufacture certainty. [R44, S32]',
'Reflection should identify a specific strength, a gap, a revision and a responsible next step. It should also acknowledge when the evidence is insufficient. A contribution log or attractive portfolio does not establish inclusion, accountability or causal development impact. Use findings from the simulated exercise to revise responsibilities, access arrangements and the review date. Record what would need to be observed under approved supervision before judging real delivery. Keep the proposed study duration open to learner piloting and accessibility review, because a timetable alone does not show that people can complete the learning effectively. [S31, S32]',
'Recognition has separate gates. A course badge requires an approved course and a verified ParaLearn course-CBT pass. School certification requires all approved school courses, a passed final mixed ParaLearn exam, human written marking, independent moderation and authorised signatures under approved arrangements. Head of Learning details and signature are pending; the Founder is Prof. Mina Ogbanga. A perfect offline result cannot certify a learner. Award decisions must use authorised records and approved assessment arrangements. Keep fictional teaching records separate from learner, enrolment and credential data, and do not invent approvals to complete the exercise. [S31]'],
'An invented portfolio contains a schedule and rehearsal notes. Its author calls it a completed placement and requests a school certificate after a perfect offline quiz.',
'Write a bounded handover and recognition statement identifying actual simulation outputs, unresolved dependencies and the distinct approval/provider/human-review gates.',
'The portfolio demonstrates an introductory exercise only. Keep real placement, provider verification, academic approval, human moderation and signatures unresolved until actually established.', ['S31','S32','R44'],[
('Sending a handover does not establish accepted responsibility.','A fictional handover is emailed without acknowledgement. What remains uncertain?','Whether a suitable authorised role accepted the next task.',['That all work is complete.','That the recipient agreed to every action.','That an email grants accreditation.'],'Transmission and accepted responsibility differ.'),
('Participation counts do not prove all needs were met.','Nine invented attendances are recorded. What cannot be inferred alone?','That the programme achieved its development outcomes.',['That a count was recorded.','That missing perspectives may matter.','That outputs should be defined.'],'Attendance does not demonstrate benefit or inclusion.'),
('Reflection should lead to a bounded next action.','A review says improve everything without an owner. What is more useful?','A specific gap, feasible revision, responsible role and review point.',['An unsupported impact claim.','A fabricated receipt.','Removal of all uncertainties.'],'Specific responsibility makes learning actionable.'),
('A perfect offline score cannot authorise certification.','A learner achieves every offline mark. What recognition limit remains?','Approved curriculum, verified provider results, human review and signatures are still required.',['A certificate must issue automatically.','The provider ID can be invented.','Moderation is unnecessary after full marks.'],'Offline contracts intentionally cannot certify.'),
('Course badges and school certificates have different gates.','An approved course CBT pass is verified. What does that alone not establish?','Completion of all school courses and the final moderated mixed exam for certification.',['That a course result can be checked.','That course approval matters.','That authorised records are needed.'],'School certification has additional curriculum, exam and signature requirements.')])])
